I took a close look at your screenshots and code, and what’s happening is that in OL 7 the shared TileQueue is de-duplicating requests by the tile’s key (tileCoord z/x/y) alone. Since each of your 20 WMTS layers requests the very same tile coordinates (just with different TIME parameters), the queue thinks they’re all the *same* tile and only lets the first one load until it’s “done,” then moves on to the next. In OL 6 the queue keying was effectively per‐tile‐instance (so all 20 would fire in parallel), but in OL 7 the default key function is simply `tile.getKey()`.

The fix is to give each tile instance its own unique queue key, rather than collapsing by coordinate. We can do that by switching from `tile.getKey()` to `getUid(tile)` (which returns a unique identifier per tile object) in the TileQueue’s key function and in its loading cache.

Below are three simple *SEARCH/REPLACE* edits against **src/ol/TileQueue.js** to:

1. import `getUid`
2. use `getUid(tile)` instead of `tile.getKey()` in the queue key function
3. use `getUid(tile)` instead of `tile.getKey()` in the loading‐cache cleanup

Each patch includes ~3 lines of context so the editors can find the right spot.

